## The idea

Merge the two trees **key by key**. At every node the merged value is just the sum of the two values. Children that exist on both sides are merged the same way (recursion); a child on only one side is copied whole. We build a **new** tree, so neither input is changed. For very deep trees, the same idea runs with an explicit stack instead of recursion.

## Step by step

1. If one tree is null, return a copy of the other.
2. The roots must have the same key; otherwise throw.
3. Create the merged node with value = s.Value + t.Value.
4. For each child of s: if t has the same key, merge the two children; otherwise copy s's child.
5. For each child of t that s did not have: copy it.
6. Iterative version: push (sNode, tNode, mergedNode) on a stack and process pairs until the stack is empty — no recursion depth limit.

## Worked example

**Input:** s = root(10){ a(6){ x(2) }, b(1) }   t = root(12){ a(4), c(5) }

| Step | What happens |
|---|---|
| root | both have root → merged root = 10 + 12 = 22 |
| child a | in both → merge: 6 + 4 = 10 |
| a → x | only in s → copy x(2) |
| child b | only in s → copy b(1) |
| child c | only in t → copy c(5) |

**Result:** root(22){ a(10){ x(2) }, b(1), c(5) } — and s and t are unchanged

## Cost

O(n_s + n_t) time — every node of both trees is visited once. O(n_s + n_t) memory for the new tree.

## The code (C#, compiled and tested against every part of the question)

```csharp
// TreeNode is provided (Models.cs): Key, Value, Children (Dictionary<string, TreeNode>).
public static class MetaTreeMerge {

    // Recursive merge. Builds a new tree; never changes s or t.
    public static TreeNode Merge(TreeNode s, TreeNode t) {
        if (s == null) return Clone(t);
        if (t == null) return Clone(s);
        if (s.Key != t.Key) throw new ArgumentException("Root keys differ: " + s.Key + " vs " + t.Key);

        TreeNode merged = new TreeNode(s.Key, s.Value + t.Value);

        foreach (KeyValuePair<string, TreeNode> pair in s.Children) {
            TreeNode other;
            if (t.Children.TryGetValue(pair.Key, out other)) {
                merged.Children[pair.Key] = Merge(pair.Value, other);   // in both: merge
            } else {
                merged.Children[pair.Key] = Clone(pair.Value);          // only in s: copy
            }
        }
        foreach (KeyValuePair<string, TreeNode> pair in t.Children) {
            if (!merged.Children.ContainsKey(pair.Key)) {
                merged.Children[pair.Key] = Clone(pair.Value);          // only in t: copy
            }
        }
        return merged;
    }

    // One unit of work for the iterative version: merge sNode and tNode into `into`.
    private class Job {
        public TreeNode S;
        public TreeNode T;
        public TreeNode Into;
        public Job(TreeNode s, TreeNode t, TreeNode into) { S = s; T = t; Into = into; }
    }

    // Same result, but with our own stack, so a 60,000-deep tree cannot overflow the call stack.
    public static TreeNode MergeIterative(TreeNode s, TreeNode t) {
        if (s == null) return Clone(t);
        if (t == null) return Clone(s);
        if (s.Key != t.Key) throw new ArgumentException("Root keys differ: " + s.Key + " vs " + t.Key);

        TreeNode root = new TreeNode(s.Key, s.Value + t.Value);
        Stack<Job> work = new Stack<Job>();
        work.Push(new Job(s, t, root));

        while (work.Count > 0) {
            Job job = work.Pop();
            foreach (KeyValuePair<string, TreeNode> pair in job.S.Children) {
                TreeNode other;
                if (job.T.Children.TryGetValue(pair.Key, out other)) {
                    TreeNode child = new TreeNode(pair.Key, pair.Value.Value + other.Value);
                    job.Into.Children[pair.Key] = child;
                    work.Push(new Job(pair.Value, other, child));          // merge its children later
                } else {
                    job.Into.Children[pair.Key] = Clone(pair.Value);
                }
            }
            foreach (KeyValuePair<string, TreeNode> pair in job.T.Children) {
                if (!job.Into.Children.ContainsKey(pair.Key)) {
                    job.Into.Children[pair.Key] = Clone(pair.Value);
                }
            }
        }
        return root;
    }

    // Deep copy, also with an explicit stack so it is safe for deep branches.
    public static TreeNode Clone(TreeNode node) {
        if (node == null) return null;
        TreeNode copyRoot = new TreeNode(node.Key, node.Value);
        Stack<TreeNode[]> work = new Stack<TreeNode[]>();     // pairs: { original, copy }
        work.Push(new TreeNode[] { node, copyRoot });
        while (work.Count > 0) {
            TreeNode[] pair = work.Pop();
            foreach (KeyValuePair<string, TreeNode> child in pair[0].Children) {
                TreeNode childCopy = new TreeNode(child.Value.Key, child.Value.Value);
                pair[1].Children[child.Key] = childCopy;
                work.Push(new TreeNode[] { child.Value, childCopy });
            }
        }
        return copyRoot;
    }
}
```

